1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,125
January fifteenth, two thousand nine. New York Tracon, Westbury, New York.

2
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:10,353
Air traffic controller Patrick Harten slides into the LaGuardia departure radar seat.

3
00:00:10,353 --> 00:00:12,532
Three twelve in the afternoon.

4
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:19,465
Fifteen quiet minutes into his shift, his radio crackles with three words no controller ever wants to hear.

5
00:00:20,599 --> 00:00:42,099
What you're about to hear is the real recording, from that exact console, at that exact minute.

6
00:00:43,233 --> 00:00:48,954
For the next few minutes, a pilot Harten has never met tells him, calmly, that both engines are gone.

7
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:53,276
"People don't survive landings on the Hudson River," Harten said later.

8
00:00:53,276 --> 00:00:55,585
"I thought it was his own death sentence."

9
00:00:56,733 --> 00:01:02,219
For the next thirty minutes, Patrick Harten believed he had just listened to a plane full of people die.

10
00:01:03,366 --> 00:01:07,728
He was wrong. This is what actually happened, in the three minutes in between.

11
00:01:08,866 --> 00:01:10,904
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

12
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:15,664
Miracle on the Hudson. What the black box actually recorded.

13
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:18,993
Chapter one. An ordinary day.

14
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:21,813
LaGuardia Airport, New York.

15
00:01:21,813 --> 00:01:27,272
US Airways flight fifteen forty-nine, an A three twenty, is bound for Charlotte.

16
00:01:27,272 --> 00:01:31,052
Nothing about the morning suggests it will be remembered.

17
00:01:32,199 --> 00:01:36,116
By two thousand nine, US Airways had one name and one operating certificate.

18
00:01:36,116 --> 00:01:45,756
But its pilots were still fighting over whose seniority actually counted, a labor war that had nothing to do with this flight, and everything to do with who was in this cockpit.

19
00:01:46,899 --> 00:01:50,019
In the left seat: Captain Chesley Sullenberger, fifty seven.

20
00:01:50,019 --> 00:02:01,110
A former Air Force fighter pilot, a glider instructor since his first year at the Academy, twenty nine years into an airline career that started before some of his passengers were born.

21
00:02:02,233 --> 00:02:05,660
In the right seat: First Officer Jeffrey Skiles, forty nine.

22
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:13,884
He'd actually been a captain, on other aircraft, before the industry gutted itself after September eleventh and bumped him back down to first officer.

23
00:02:15,033 --> 00:02:19,304
Skiles had gotten his rating on this exact aircraft two weeks earlier.

24
00:02:19,304 --> 00:02:21,083
Thirty seven hours in type.

25
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:26,422
This was, in fact, the very first trip he and Sullenberger had ever flown together.

26
00:02:27,566 --> 00:02:30,994
One hundred fifty passengers board. A lap-held infant among them.

27
00:02:30,994 --> 00:02:36,478
Three flight attendants: Doreen Welsh, thirty eight years in the job, working alone at the rear.

28
00:02:36,478 --> 00:02:38,877
Sheila Dail and Donna Dent up front.

29
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,095
This is the real recording of that departure. Nothing unusual.

30
00:02:43,095 --> 00:02:46,500
Just a tower controller, clearing one more flight off the runway.

31
00:02:47,633 --> 00:02:52,018
Three twenty four PM. Cleared for takeoff, runway four. The climb is smooth.

32
00:02:52,018 --> 00:02:59,440
Nothing about it has ever been recorded as remarkable, in any of the thousands of flights that leave LaGuardia every single day.

33
00:03:00,566 --> 00:03:02,212
Chapter two. The birds.

34
00:03:03,333 --> 00:03:09,379
Two minutes after takeoff, at two thousand eight hundred and eighteen feet, the captain says one word on the recorder.

35
00:03:09,379 --> 00:03:09,681
Birds.

36
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:16,918
A flock of Canada geese, later confirmed by DNA analysis, goes straight into both engines.

37
00:03:16,918 --> 00:03:21,406
Fan speed. Core speed. Both collapsing, within seconds of each other.

38
00:03:22,533 --> 00:03:27,376
In the cockpit, Sullenberger takes the controls from Skiles. "My aircraft," he says.

39
00:03:27,376 --> 00:03:35,945
Skiles starts running the checklist for a dual engine failure, a procedure almost no airline crew has ever had to use for real.

40
00:03:37,066 --> 00:04:22,066
This is that same real recording again. Listen to how fast the calm breaks.

41
00:04:23,199 --> 00:04:27,141
Mayday, mayday, mayday. This is Cactus fifteen thirty-nine. Hit birds.

42
00:04:27,141 --> 00:04:31,477
We've lost thrust in both engines. We're turning back towards LaGuardia.

43
00:04:31,477 --> 00:04:36,600
Under the stress of the moment, he even misstates his own flight number.

44
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:59,733
Harten offers him LaGuardia's runway thirteen. Then thirty one. Then four.

45
00:04:59,733 --> 00:05:13,733
Real audio, again, the same call continuing.

46
00:05:14,866 --> 00:05:16,991
"Unable." Each time, the same word.

47
00:05:16,991 --> 00:05:21,594
A jet with no engines does not turn around and land like normal.

48
00:05:21,594 --> 00:05:24,427
Every option Harten offers, Sullenberger has to refuse.

49
00:05:25,566 --> 00:05:29,772
Then Sullenberger says the sentence that ends the search for a runway completely.

50
00:05:30,899 --> 00:05:50,664
"We may end up in the Hudson." He asks about Teterboro, in New Jersey.

51
00:05:50,664 --> 00:05:57,723
Harten clears him toward it.

52
00:05:57,723 --> 00:06:18,899
And then, seconds later, the real recording catches the moment radar loses the aircraft completely.

53
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:21,992
Chapter three. The decision.

54
00:06:23,133 --> 00:06:25,311
Sullenberger later called it 208 seconds.

55
00:06:25,311 --> 00:06:31,484
From bird strike to committing to the river, the report's own timestamps put it closer to 213.

56
00:06:31,484 --> 00:06:37,657
Either way: under four minutes to decide, correctly, with a plane full of people, and be right.

57
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:41,170
No engines. No runway close enough.

58
00:06:41,170 --> 00:06:48,281
A glider instructor's entire second career had, without either of them knowing it, been training for exactly this.

59
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:56,278
Skiles is still running the dual-engine checklist, a procedure built for altitude, not for a jet with three minutes left in the air.

60
00:06:56,278 --> 00:06:58,073
There isn't time to finish it.

61
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:01,124
The last words on the recorder.

62
00:07:01,124 --> 00:07:06,898
Both men, together, bracing a plane full of strangers for a landing neither has ever practiced for real.

63
00:07:08,033 --> 00:07:09,914
Chapter four. The Hudson.

64
00:07:11,033 --> 00:07:17,568
About eight and a half miles from LaGuardia, the airplane touches down on the Hudson River.

65
00:07:17,568 --> 00:07:20,836
Air temperature: nineteen degrees. Water temperature: forty one.

66
00:07:20,836 --> 00:07:24,512
Cold enough to kill, on its own, in minutes.

67
00:07:25,633 --> 00:07:31,045
All one hundred fifty five people aboard evacuate onto the wings, and into the slide-rafts.

68
00:07:31,045 --> 00:07:33,571
Structural debris tears into the rear cabin.

69
00:07:33,571 --> 00:07:37,179
Flight attendant Doreen Welsh, alone back there, is seriously hurt.

70
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:43,004
She kept directing passengers toward the exits as the water rose around her feet.

71
00:07:43,004 --> 00:07:45,693
She evacuated last, after everyone else was off.

72
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:52,219
The final tally, from the official investigation: one hundred fifty five aboard.

73
00:07:52,219 --> 00:07:56,707
Five seriously injured. Ninety five with minor injuries. Zero dead.

74
00:07:57,833 --> 00:07:59,871
Chapter five. The rescue.

75
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,494
This is real footage.

76
00:08:02,494 --> 00:08:10,717
A US Coast Guard harbor camera, running that exact afternoon, catching the plane itself, down in the river, minutes after the ditching.

77
00:08:11,866 --> 00:08:16,291
The ferry Thomas Jefferson, captained by Vincent Lombardi, is already crossing the river.

78
00:08:16,291 --> 00:08:20,036
He reaches the plane in roughly three and a half minutes.

79
00:08:20,036 --> 00:08:23,099
Before he even arrives, he warns his own crew.

80
00:08:24,233 --> 00:08:29,562
"I saw people walking out from the plane," Lombardi said, "and it kind of looked like they were walking on water."

81
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:32,374
Fourteen NY Waterway ferries respond.

82
00:08:32,374 --> 00:08:37,399
Between them, they pull roughly one hundred forty three people out of the freezing river.

83
00:08:37,399 --> 00:08:42,089
The Coast Guard and FDNY get the rest. All one hundred fifty five, alive.

84
00:08:43,233 --> 00:08:45,741
Chapter six. No verdict needed.

85
00:08:46,866 --> 00:08:48,872
Investigators needed the aircraft itself.

86
00:08:48,872 --> 00:08:56,897
Days later, real US Coast Guard footage shows the actual airframe, lifted from the river at night, headed for examination.

87
00:08:58,033 --> 00:09:08,952
The National Transportation Safety Board's official finding: the ingestion of large birds into each engine, resulting in an almost total loss of thrust, and the subsequent ditching on the Hudson River.

88
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:15,096
Here's what almost never happens after a US air disaster. Not one passenger.

89
00:09:15,096 --> 00:09:16,634
Not one crew member.

90
00:09:16,634 --> 00:09:20,862
Sued the airline, or the plane's manufacturer, over the accident itself.

91
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:32,919
An aviation attorney who specializes in exactly these cases called the total absence of litigation "amazing." There was no death, and very little to argue was anyone's fault.

92
00:09:34,066 --> 00:09:36,535
The Board issued thirty four safety recommendations.

93
00:09:36,535 --> 00:09:44,293
Engines now have to prove they can survive bird strikes at low, reduced-power speeds, exactly the scenario that brought this plane down.

94
00:09:44,293 --> 00:09:47,467
That rule took the FAA fourteen years to finalize.

95
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,544
Around New York's airports, wildlife-management programs expanded.

96
00:09:51,544 --> 00:10:00,799
Over seventy thousand birds removed from the area in the years since, an unglamorous, ongoing consequence of three minutes over the Hudson.

97
00:10:01,933 --> 00:10:04,049
Chapter seven. Where they are now.

98
00:10:05,166 --> 00:10:09,117
Sullenberger retired from the airline in twenty ten, after thirty years.

99
00:10:09,117 --> 00:10:18,097
He later served briefly as America's ambassador to the International Civil Aviation Organization, and has spent years since as a public voice for aviation safety.

100
00:10:19,233 --> 00:10:30,962
Skiles, the first officer with thirty seven hours in that cockpit, went on to represent tens of thousands of pilots on safety policy, and only retired in twenty twenty four, fifteen years after that flight.

101
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:41,157
Doreen Welsh, hurt in the rear cabin, made that flight her last before retiring, and has spoken openly since about living with the fear it left behind.

102
00:10:41,157 --> 00:10:44,848
Sheila Dail retired exactly ten years later, on the anniversary itself.

103
00:10:45,966 --> 00:10:51,782
Patrick Harten, the man who spent thirty minutes believing he'd heard everyone die, eventually left the FAA too.

104
00:10:51,782 --> 00:10:58,243
His own account of that afternoon is now part of the public record, in his own sworn words to Congress.

105
00:10:59,366 --> 00:11:13,159
As recently as this year, one of the passengers aboard that day was still speaking publicly, fighting alongside flight attendants for stronger cabin-crew staffing rules, and naming Welsh, Dail, and Dent by name as the reason she survived.

106
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:23,208
And the aircraft itself, the actual airframe that carried all one hundred fifty five of them onto that river, has sat on public display in a Charlotte museum since twenty eleven.

107
00:11:24,333 --> 00:11:28,042
What stays with me about this one isn't the landing. It's Patrick Harten.

108
00:11:29,166 --> 00:11:35,671
Thirty minutes believing he'd just listened to a plane full of strangers die, before a colleague finally told him: they survived.

109
00:11:35,671 --> 00:11:40,007
He didn't believe it, until he turned on a television and saw it himself.

110
00:11:41,133 --> 00:11:45,558
Every other story on this channel ends with an investigation finding someone to blame.

111
00:11:45,558 --> 00:11:47,138
This one didn't need to.

112
00:11:47,138 --> 00:11:50,615
Two tired men, a checklist, and three minutes, were simply enough.

113
00:11:51,733 --> 00:11:54,019
One hundred fifty five people walked away.

114
00:11:54,019 --> 00:11:58,264
The aircraft that carried them never flew again. It still sits in Charlotte.

115
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:07,354
If a crew that got everything right pulled you in, wait until you see one that got almost everything wrong, our video on Colgan Air thirty four oh seven.

116
00:12:07,354 --> 00:12:08,726
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

